  "openmp_diagnosis": {
    "note": "turing serve decoded at 80 ms a token where turing-cpu and a Python loop over Llama.step took 60. ggml and torch share one libgomp, which keeps a team of threads per calling thread; the server ran libllama calls on asyncio.to_thread workers and torch on the event loop's thread, so the process held more OpenMP threads than its 16 CPUs and libgomp stopped spinning between parallel regions (OMP_WAIT_POLICY=passive on the main thread: 88 ms; =active with a second team: 797 ms). Fewer ggml threads did not help (15: 11.1 tok/s, 14: 11.0, 16: 12.1). Fix: every libllama call on one engine-owned thread, torch.set_num_threads(1) in turing serve --backend libllama. Lines: ms per decode step, Qwen3-8B Q4_K_M, 16 threads.",
    "generate_main_vs_worker_thread": [
      "generate, n_ctx 4096, main thread              64.1 ms/step (median)",
      "generate, n_ctx 4096, worker thread            81.1 ms/step (median)",
      "generate, n_ctx 8192, main thread              62.6 ms/step (median)",
      "generate, n_ctx 8192, worker thread            81.9 ms/step (median)",
      "generate, n_ctx 8192, after the server imports   64.9 ms/step (median)",
      "generate on the engine's Llama (as turing serve)   67.0 ms/step (median)"
    ],
    "openmp_env": [
      "(default)                                main thread      70.9 ms/step",
